          %p (…)
          %h2#StructureAndCategoryTheory
            Bourbaki’s notion of
            %em structure
            and the relation to category theory
          %p
            A central point of view of Bourbaki is the emphasis of mathematics as the study of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emmy+Noether"} Emmy Noether
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emil+Artin"} Emil Artin
            %em Moderne Algebra
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Corry"} Corry
          %p
            However, Bourbaki did not embrace
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structuralism"} structuralism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+properties"} universal properties
            treated in category theory in fact first appeared in early editions of Bourbaki. Instead, Bourbaki proposed its own formalization of the notion of “structure” (in the book
            = succeed ")," do
              %em Theory of Sets
            = succeed "&rbrack;," do
              %a{:href => "#Corry"} Corry
            %em Theory of Sets
            when they are considered in their intended logical order).
          %p
            Discussion of this point can be found in &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#CTList"}> CTList
            \&amp;rbrack;. There
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"} Colin McLarty
            writes:
          %blockquote
            %p Bourbaki’s first publication was
            %p
              Bourbaki, N. [1939]:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Théorie des ensembles, Fascicules de résultats
            %p It is very sketchy on “structures,” and uses no notion of mapping between structures except isomorphisms. Their actual theory of structures first appeared in
            %p
              Bourbaki, N. [1957]:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Théorie des ensembles
            %p That theory was a rear-guard action meant to give an alternative to category theory. As i mentioned before, Weil was citing the categorical idea, and thinking about finding an in-house alternative to it, already in 1951. By 1957 Grothendieck, and Cartier, and Chevalley, probably Dieudonné, and others, all saw that category theory was more agile than these structure, simpler, and more to the point, plus it had a natural “higher order” aspect in the theory of functors which was actually more useful in practice than categories alone.
            %p Cartier has justly said it would have been a huge job to formulate all Bourbaki’s ideas in terms of categories and functors. It would have called for a lot of ideas which were only invented in the coming years.
            %p
              It was relatively easy to give Bourbaki’s theory of structures – because it never really worked at all even for Bourbaki’s purposes (as Corry documents in detail). Naturally it is easier to give an unusable theory of structures than to work out the ways categories and functors would actually be used. &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://permalink.gmane.org/gmane.science.mathematics.categories/7298"}> McLarty, Category Theory List 2012
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://www.bourbaki.fr/"} website
              of the group
          %p Accounts of the history of the Bourbaki project include the following:
          %ul
            %li#Dieudonne
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+Dieudonn%C3%A9"}> Jean Dieudonné
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The work of Nicolas Bourbaki
                %strong 77
                (1970) 134-145 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=252179"}> MR252179
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.2307/2317325"} doi
            %li
              %p
                Dennis Guedj,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Nicholas Bourbaki, Collective Mathematician - An Interview with Claude Chevalley
                %strong 7
                no. 2 (1985) pp.18-22.
            %li
              %p
                Bourbaki’s biography at math tutor: the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www-groups.dcs.st-and.ac.uk/~history/HistTopics/Bourbaki_1.html"} pre-war years
                %a{:href => "http://www-groups.dcs.st-and.ac.uk/~history/HistTopics/Bourbaki_2.html"} after-war years
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Armand+Borel"}> Armand Borel
                \:
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ega-math.narod.ru/Bbaki/Bourb3.htm"} 25 years with Bourbaki
            %li
              %p
                Amir D. Aczel,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em The artist and the mathematician
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2339913"} MR2008h:01020
                %a{:href => "http://www.siam.org/news/news.php?id=1078"} SIAM review
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a{:href => "http://mathdoc.emath.fr/archives-bourbaki/"} Bourbaki archives
                contain many draughts (and discussions thereof) of Bourbaki’s texts
          %p
            Discussion of Bourbaki’s notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} mathematical structure
            and the relation (or not) to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %ul#Aubin
            %li
              %p
                Jean-Michel Kantor,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Bourbaki’s Structures and Structuralism
                = succeed ":1" do
                  %strong 33
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s00283-010-9173-4"} doi
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leo+Corry"}> Leo Corry
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematical Structures from Hilbert to Bourbaki: The Evolution of an Image of Mathematics
                %em Changing Images of Mathematics in History. From the French Revolution to the new Millenium
                Harwood Academic Publishers (2001) 167-186 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.routledge.com/Changing-Images-in-Mathematics-From-the-French-Revolution-to-the-New-Millennium/Bottazini-Dalmedico/p/book/9780415868273"}> ISBN:9780415868273
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.leocorry.com/_files/ugd/e6fef7_15c7077b08ec4f249fb40eb961256ced.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Corry
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leo+Corry"}> Leo Corry
                \:
                %em Nicolas Bourbaki: Theory of structures
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.tau.ac.il/~corry/publications/articles/pdf/bourbaki-structures.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack; Chapter 7 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modern Algebra and the Rise of Mathematical Structures
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-0348-7917-0"} doi:10.1007/978-3-0348-7917-0
            %li#Kroemer06
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ralf+Kr%C3%B6mer"}> Ralf Krömer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em La « machine de Grothendieck », se fonde-t-elle seulement sur des vocables métamathématiques? Bourbaki et les catégories au cours des années cinquante
                %strong 12
                111–154 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/RHM_2006__12_1_119_0/"}> Numdam
                )
            %li#CTList
              %p
                Category Theory List, Staffan Angere,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://web.archive.org/web/20160427073919/http://comments.gmane.org/gmane.science.mathematics.categories/7283"} Bourbaki &amp; category theory
            %li
              %p
                David Aubin,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Withering Immortality of Nicolas Bourbaki: A Cultural Connector at the Confluence of Mathematics, Structuralism, and the Oulipo in France
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.jussieu.fr/~daubin/publis/1997.pdf"} pdf
          %ul
            %li
              Alberto Peruzzi, Silvano Zipoli Caiani (eds.),
              = succeed "," do
                %em Structures Mères: Semantics, Mathematics, and Cognitive Science
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-51821-9"} doi
